\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N$, $\mathbb Z$, $\mathbb Q$, $\mathbb R$, and $\mathbb C$ denote the natural numbers, integers, rationals, reals, and complex numbers, respectively. All additional parameters and hypotheses are specified in the question.


\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For the short-range quantum Heisenberg ferromagnet in dimension $d\geq 3$, prove that sufficiently low temperature produces long-range order; specifically, prove that $\lim_{\Lambda\nearrow\mathbb{Z}^d}|\Lambda|^{-2}\sum_{x,y\in\Lambda}\langle\widetilde S_x\cdot\widetilde S_y\rangle_{T,\Lambda}>0$ for all sufficiently small $T>0$.
\par\smallskip\textit{Formulation references:} \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}, \href{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2025/7}{[S2]}.

\subsection{Short English statement}
For the short-range quantum Heisenberg ferromagnet in dimension $d\geq 3$, prove that sufficiently low temperature produces long-range order; specifically, prove that $\lim_{\Lambda\nearrow\mathbb{Z}^d}|\Lambda|^{-2}\sum_{x,y\in\Lambda}\langle\widetilde S_x\cdot\widetilde S_y\rangle_{T,\Lambda}>0$ for all sufficiently small $T>0$.

