Pinned exchange returns are compared with the one-magnon walk. Uniform sparse-pin and long-return remainder bounds isolate finite returns, whose small-momentum asymptotics produce the Bloch coefficient; volume, zero-field derivative, and temperature limits are taken in that order. Spin slots and exchange loops translate a simultaneous pin test into return-path estimates. Sparse obstacles are bypassed by local diffusion flows, and heat-flow leakage bounds close a pinning bootstrap, producing a magnetized thermodynamic equilibrium state.

Capacity comparison controls pinned return cycles on a period. Sparse-obstacle propagation and an ideal-potential profile identify comparison sources whose cancellation improves the error beyond the leading Bloch term, allowing the next dispersion coefficient to survive. Exchange-cycle pins and zero-free traces control torus comparisons at vanishing field. Removing the field across spatial scales and suppressing replica cross terms identifies the moment law; pressure tangents determine its radius without assigning joint measurements to noncommuting components.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The d=3 Bloch law establishes low-temperature order for finite-range quantum Heisenberg ferromagnets, but the problem statement quantifies every d>=3 and an explicit zero-field averaged-correlation thermodynamic limit. The theorem constructs a magnetized translation-invariant zero-field KMS state for all d>=3, but does not itself assert the problem statement exact thermodynamic limit of averaged finite-volume zero-field correlations. The latter is separately quantified, so it is not silently identified with state existence. The d=3 nearest-neighbor low-temperature expansion strengthens positive magnetization in that case, without providing the problem statement in every d>=3 or its full averaged-limit formulation. The theorem proves the positive zero-field averaged-correlation limit m\textasciicircum{}2 for all spins at sufficiently low positive temperature in d=3. The problem statement requires the corresponding assertion in every dimension d>=3. This entry records partial support; complete coverage of the question is not established by this catalogue.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 271, the selected theorem constructs low-temperature zero-field magnetized KMS states for all dimensions at least three and all positive spins. The Bloch expansion, lattice correction, and spherical moment law are separate unselected companions. The listed Comparator files are \texttt{Heisenberg.lean}. The inventory does not supply a named declaration for these Comparator entries. This site has not executed the Lean code. The scope descriptions do not certify the remaining manuscript arguments.

